1. Confirm you’re in the right VPC and subnet

bash
TOKEN=$(curl -s -X PUT "http://169.254.169.254/latest/api/token" -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token-ttl-seconds: 60")
curl -s -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/network/interfaces/macs/ | head -1 | xargs -I{} curl -s -H "X-aws-ec2-metadata-token: $TOKEN" http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/network/interfaces/macs/{}vpc-id

If that metadata call doesn’t work in CloudShell, skip it and check the VPC ID in the console instead. It should be vpc-0758ef1be26b0b173.

2. Resolve the ALB name

bash
nslookup internal-ecs-express-gateway-alb-b8810e7d-2057591608.us-east-1.elb.amazonaws.com

You should get 10.x.x.x addresses. Public IPs mean the lookup is going out to the internet, so the environment isn’t resolving through the VPC.

3. Send the request with the Host header

Get the full host value from rule 44990 on the HTTPS:443 listener, then run:

bash
curl -sk -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" \
  -H "Host: <full-host-from-rule-44990>" \
  https://internal-ecs-express-gateway-alb-b8810e7d-2057591608.us-east-1.elb.amazonaws.com/

Reading the result:

200: The ALB forwarded to the container and the app answered. The UI is working.
404: The Host header doesn’t match rule 44990. Re-copy it exactly.
502 / 503: The target is unhealthy. Check that the container listens on port 5000.
Timeout: The environment’s security group can’t reach the ALB on 443. Fix the ALB’s inbound rule as described earlier.

